        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hammurabi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1810 BCE – c. 1750 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1810 BCE by convention (the date is unknown); son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a>, of the Amorite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1750 BCE, in the forty-third year of his reign, apparently of illness or perhaps of old age</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> (c. 1792 – c. 1750 BCE); later also king of the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, king of the entire Amorite land and king of the four regions of the world</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>; the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>; uniting almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>; more than 200 surviving letters to his officials; canals, city walls and temple works; edicts of debt remission</span></div>
        </div>
