    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hammurabi inherited a kingdom of limited territory with numerous rivals and, in a few years in the second half of his reign, eliminated the major kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>.</li><li>His law code, written towards the end of the reign and divided by modern historians into 282 provisions, is the longest legal text to survive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, though its nature and function are debated.</li><li>The empire did not outlast him: his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> lost the south to revolts, and later ages remembered Hammurabi less as a conqueror than as the ideal lawgiver.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hammurabi refused to swear his oath of alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> on the 25th of the month, apparently a day considered unlucky for invoking the moon god Sin, and took it three days later before his council.</p></aside>
    </section>

