    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1792 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Hammurabi succeeds his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and in his first year issues an edict of debt remission</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1775 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Samsi-Addu dies and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a> disintegrates; in the struggle that follows Hammurabi allies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1763 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> he takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, brings out its king Rim-Sin alive and annexes the kingdom, gaining the whole lower Mesopotamian plain</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1761 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He turns on his former ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> is captured and then destroyed between 1761 and 1759 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1750 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Hammurabi dies in the forty-third year of his reign and is succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sin-muballit.jpg" alt="Sin-muballit" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_tablet_and_its_sealed_clay_envelope._Legal_document,_listing_of_land_and_their_distribution_to_several_sons._From_Sippar,_Iraq._Old-Babylonian_period._Reign_of_Sin-Muballit,_1812-1793_BCE._Vorderasiatisches_Museum,_Berlin,_Germany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hammurabi was born into the Amorite dynasty that had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> since around 1880 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a> made a secondary city without a notable history the centre of a kingdom dominating neighbours such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, Kutha, Dilbat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marad">Marad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>. His father and predecessor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a>. The date of Hammurabi's birth is unknown, as is his age at his accession: given a reign of 43 years he may have been very young, though he may also have been in his twenties or even thirties. His family is very poorly known. None of his wives is documented, no brother is named, and one sister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltani">Iltani</a>, became a nadītum, a nun devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> at Sippar.</p><p>Since the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> in 2004 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> had been divided among kingdoms ruled by Amorite dynasties. In the south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, under the long-reigning Rim-Sin, had annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and was Babylon's most threatening rival. To the north-east lay the expansionist kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> in the Diyala valley, and in the north Samsi-Addu, a member of the same Amorite clan as the kings of Babylon, had built from Ekallatum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> what historians call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>. The meaning of the name Hammurabi, borne by other Amorite kings of the time, is debated; it is most often read as “the ancestor is great”. All dates of the reign are conventional: the most commonly used system, the Middle chronology, places it from 1792 to 1750 BCE, and others would move it by half a century or a century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hammurabi succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a> in 1792 BCE. In his first year he issued an edict of debt remission, commemorated in the name of his second year as the year in which he “established law and justice in his land”. The first two decades of the reign are poorly documented. The names of its early years refer mainly to public works and offerings to deities. The seventh commemorates the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, clearly a short-lived raid, since both cities stayed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>; the tenth a victory over Malgium, won with the support of Samsi-Addu and the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, though that city remained independent.</p><p>The death of Samsi-Addu in 1775 BCE was followed by the rapid disintegration of his kingdom and a struggle for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> between Eshnunna and the new king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, with whom Hammurabi ultimately formed an alliance. From this point the letters found at Mari record events in some detail. The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Mari, attacked and subjugated Eshnunna, but did not stop there, and a coalition of Babylon, Mari and other kingdoms formed against it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> tried to sow discord between Hammurabi and Rim-Sin of Larsa; the two kings uncovered the ruse and allied, though Rim-Sin contributed little or nothing. The Elamites, who threatened the north of the Babylonian kingdom, were repelled with great difficulty, thanks to troops from Mari and other northern kingdoms. The alliance with Mari had been delayed by a dispute over the city of Hit, which was set aside unresolved.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="Code of Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bolstered by the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, Hammurabi launched a lightning war against Rim-Sin with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>'s meagre support and alleged attacks on his territory, and annexed the kingdom in 1763 BCE, gaining the whole lower Mesopotamian plain. A victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, Shubartum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a> is commemorated in the name of his 32nd year. He then made war on Zimri-Lim: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> was captured and destroyed between 1761 and 1759 BCE, and Malgium suffered a similar fate. Later year names record the defeat of a northern coalition and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> from the conquered lands; in the process he established his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. His titles grew from “king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>” to “king of the four regions” of the world.</p><p>The king dug canals, among them one completed in his 33rd year and named “Hammurabi (brings) abundance to the people”, rebuilt the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> and restored sanctuaries. Just over 200 of his letters survive, more than for any other Mesopotamian king; about a hundred are addressed to Sin-iddinam, governor of Larsa, on irrigation, troops, taxes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour and legal cases, and they show the king involving himself directly in provincial affairs and answering petitions from his subjects.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>, written towards the end of the reign and known mainly from a large stele, has a prologue, an epilogue and what modern historians divide into 282 provisions on judicial practice, property, agriculture, finance, family law, assault, the professions and slaves. It is not the oldest Mesopotamian collection of laws, but it is the longest known.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshnunna.jpg" alt="Eshnunna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman_head_Tell_Asmas_Louvre_AO12434.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hammurabi's chief adversaries were the kings he had treated with: Rim-Sin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, an ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>; Silli-Sin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, a commoner enthroned by that city's elites, to whom Hammurabi married a daughter shortly before defeating him; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, whose troops had helped him against Elam and Larsa. The rift with Mari appears to have come from Hammurabi's ambitions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar-mountains">Jebel Sinjar</a> region. The Mari letters show a skilled negotiator who used his allies while giving them as little as possible, and a ruler whom K. Radner describes as “a volatile and at times rather threatening personality”.</p><p>Beyond the image of a just king that he wished to project, his conquests caused widespread devastation, and P. Michalowski points out that he can be viewed as a “tyrant”. He ordered the destruction of the palace at Mari and deported the defeated, as after the Mutiabal revolt. Such acts were not unusual for his time, however, and nothing indicates that he was more brutal than other kings of his era. Some scholars have read his attention to small matters as the mark of a petty ruler, a judgement M. Van De Mieroop calls mistaken. The status of the laws is also disputed: many see them as a scholarly exercise by scribes that describes the law rather than prescribing it, and there is no conclusive proof that judges applied them; others treat them as general solutions drawn from particular cases. According to D. Charpin the empire was probably less the result of a deliberate policy than of the skilful exploitation of successive opportunities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna.jpg" alt="Samsu-iluna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Record_of_the_sale_of_land_in_the_reign_of_Samsu-Iluna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Budge, E. A. Wallis (Ernest Alfred Wallis), Sir, 1857-1934; King, L. W. (Leonard William), 1869-1919, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> archives leaves the last decade of the reign poorly documented. Year names record the defeat of a coalition of northern kings, among them those of Shubartum, Turukkum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, in the 37th year, and another victory in Shubartum in the 39th. According to D. Charpin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, already greatly weakened, appears to have been destroyed by floods mentioned in the name of the 38th year and by a shift in the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala River</a>, and was abandoned. The prologue of the Code, dated to the end of the reign, lists the cities then under Babylonian control, ending with Mari, Tuttul, Eshnunna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in the north.</p><p>Hammurabi appears to have been physically weakened from his fortieth year. R. Pientka-Hinz has suggested that the works of that year at the sanctuary of Nergal, god of the Underworld, indicate a king already ill and seeking the favour of the god who would soon receive him. A letter of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> to the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> seems to say that the king was seriously ill and his death imminent, though a break in the tablet prevents the sentence from being fully reconstructed. Samsu-iluna appears to have been ruling at that time, perhaps as regent, and prepared his accession by remitting debts, a measure typically taken by a new monarch. Hammurabi died around 1750 BCE, in the forty-third year of his reign, apparently of illness or perhaps of old age; one text dates the change of reign to the tenth day of the fifth month of the Babylonian year. Samsu-iluna succeeded him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By Hammurabi's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> was the dominant power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, but the unification was brief. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> lost the former territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> to revolts and gained nothing lasting in the north, and the dynasty, pressed by the First Sealand dynasty and by Kassite dynasties, was ended by a Hittite attack in 1595 BCE. Hammurabi can nevertheless be considered to have laid the foundations for a unified Lower Mesopotamia, reducing the old royal capitals to provincial towns.</p><p>He was declared a god in his own lifetime, and the personal name Hammurabi-ili, “Hammurabi is my god”, became common. Writings from shortly after his death commemorate him for victory, peace and justice; later the lawgiver came to overshadow the conqueror. Scribes went on copying his laws for centuries as writing exercises, the kings of Suhum claimed him as an ancestor more than a thousand years later, and a Neo-Babylonian inscription presents its king as a second Hammurabi.</p><p>His name re-emerged in the mid-19th century with the excavation of Mesopotamian sites. Joachim Menant published the first collection of his texts in 1863, and in 1901–1902 a French team unearthed the stele of the Code at Susa, its inscription being translated and published at once by Jean-Vincent Scheil. An early twentieth-century identification with Amraphel of Genesis 14:1 is now largely rejected. He is one of the 23 lawgivers shown in the chamber of the U.S. House of Representatives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> placed a bronze statue of him, over 4 metres high, at the entrance to the Iraqi Parliament.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/enuma-elis">Enūma Eliš<span>c. 1100 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23<span>c. 500 BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hammurabi, sixth Amorite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> (c. 1792 – c. 1750 BCE), turned a kingdom of limited territory into the dominant power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> by conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. He is best known for his law code, the longest legal text to survive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. His empire was lost under his son.</em></p>

